'''
Lab: Grocery List Application
In this lab, you will practice using Python Objects to create a shopping list application.



Learning Outcomes
Upon completion of this assignment, you will know how to:

Follow software requirements.
Write an interactive console-based Python program.
Use f-strings to interpolate variables into output.
Store and retrieve objects from lists.
Interpolate variables into output with f-strings.
Push code changes to a Git repository.


Repo Preparation
In your mwcc-cis109 repo directory, create a new subdirectory named lab03.



Software Requirements
##1. Upon startup, the program MUST displays the following menu to the terminal window:

Welcome to Your Shopping List!
 
Please make a selection from one of the following options:
 
1. Add an item to the shopping list.
2. Display the shopping list.
3. Display the item count.
4. Display the first item in the shopping list.
5. Display the last item in the shopping list.
4. Clear the shopping list.
 
Selection: 


2. Upon selecting option #1 from the menu, the program MUST prompt the user to add an item to a Python list object called grocery_list. After the user enters an item, the program MUST add the item to grocery_list.

Item to add: 


#3. Upon selecting option #2 from the menu, the program MUST display the items in grocery_list.

['apple', 'bannana', 'cranberries']


#4. Upon selecting option #3 from the menu, the program MUST display the count of items in grocery_list using an F-String.

ITEM COUNT: 10


#5. Upon selecting option #4 from the menu, the program MUST display the first item in grocery_list using an F-String.

FIRST ITEM: apple


#6. Upon selecting option #5 from the menu, the program MUST display the last item in grocery_list using an F-String.

LAST ITEM: cranberries


7. Upon selecting option #6 from the menu, the program MUST remove all the items from grocery_list. Hint: Set the list variable to an empty list.



8. Upon entering input other than 1 through 4, the program MUST display the following message:

You entered an invalid option.
Goodbye!


9. A main program loop MAY be implemented, but it is not required for this lab.



Check In Your Code!
Stage and commit your changes and synchronize/push your code update to GitHub.



Deliverable
Submit to Blackboard the URL to the new folder on GitHub.'''